  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nTODAY AND TONIGHT...Late morning satellite imagery with surface \nobservations showed milky skies from elevated smoke, with a few low \nlevel cumulus clouds developing. Winds were out of the south, with \ntemperatures in the 80s across the region. For the rest of today, \nexpect dry conditions, as the chance for a stray shower or \nthunderstorm is only around 5 to 10 percent. Temperatures will be \nwarm again today, with highs in the upper 80s to low 90s. For \ntonight, expect the quiet weather to continue with light southerly \nwinds and lows falling to near 70.\n\nTHURSDAY AND FRIDAY...This remains the main period of interest, as \nan upper level wave will approach from the Dakotas, and slowly move \nsoutheast across the region, drawing up the high PWAT air (near 2 \ninches) that is currently located over Nebraska and Kansas. \nConfidence remains high that this slow-moving system will produce \npockets of heavy rain with the potential for efficient, tropical \nrain processes, as indicated by the QPF output from various models. \nThe question still remains exactly where this will occur. \n\nThe latest guidance has shifted the QPF to the south and west, which \nlines up with our thinking of having the heaviest rain south/west of \nI-94, closer to the Minnesota River Valley and I-90. As for amounts, \nthe official rainfall forecast has broad brush amounts of 1 to 2 \ninches of rain in this area. However, HiRes models support isolated \namounts much higher, with pockets of 3 to 5 inches seeming plausible \nand a few reports of 6 inches or greater not out of the question. \nThe main threat would be flooding in smaller, fast responding creeks \nand streams, along with any urban areas. A Flood Watch may be needed \nif once confidence increases on where system will track. However, \ngiven the abnormally dry conditions across the region, river \nflooding along the main stems is very low.\n\nThere is also a sneaky tornado potential ahead of the surface low \ngiven the veering wind profile and low LCLs, but of course this \ndepends on the track of the surface low. Current thinking is this \ntornado risk area would be to the south, across Iowa, where there \nare increased chances for a dry slot and better instability to \ndevelop Friday afternoon.\n\nLooking ahead, this system will slowly exit to the southeast on \nFriday, with rain chances decreasing throughout the day. Most \nlocations should see dry conditions by Friday evening.\n\nTHE WEEKEND AND NEXT WEEK...No major changes to the previous \nforecast. Saturday and Sunday look fantastic weather-wise, with \nlight winds, mostly sunny skies, and temperatures in the low to mid \n80s. \n\nMonday will be the warmest day next week. If winds are light, \ndewpoints could remain in the 70s, but temperatures would be cooler. \nIf southwest winds are a bit stronger, then temperatures could climb \ninto the 90s, but dewpoints would mix out into the 50s. Either \nscenario would result in a warm/hot day, but should remain cooler \nthan Heat Advisory threshold. \n\nLooking ahead, a weak cold front will pass through the region late \nMonday into Tuesday. This will bring small chances for \nthunderstorms, along with cooler temperatures.",
